**Common Alert Handling Mistakes:**

**1. Not Waiting for Alert to Appear**
```java
// ❌ WRONG - Immediate switch
driver.findElement(By.id("alertButton")).click();
Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert(); // May throw NoAlertPresentException

// ✅ CORRECT - Wait for alert
driver.findElement(By.id("alertButton")).click();
WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
Alert alert = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent());
```

**2. Not Verifying Alert Text**
```java
// ❌ WRONG - Blindly accepting
Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();
alert.accept();

// ✅ CORRECT - Verify before accepting
Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();
String alertText = alert.getText();
Assert.assertTrue(alertText.contains("expected message"));
alert.accept();
```

**3. Trying to Locate Alert as WebElement**
```java
// ❌ WRONG - Alerts cannot be found this way
WebElement alert = driver.findElement(By.id("alert")); // Won't work!

// ✅ CORRECT - Use switchTo().alert()
Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();
```

**4. Not Handling NoAlertPresentException**
```java
// ❌ WRONG - No error handling
Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();

// ✅ CORRECT - Proper error handling
try {
    WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(5));
    Alert alert = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent());
    alert.accept();
} catch (TimeoutException e) {
    System.out.println("Alert did not appear");
}
```

**5. Using sendKeys() on Non-Prompt Alerts**
```java
// ❌ WRONG - sendKeys only works on prompts
Alert simpleAlert = driver.switchTo().alert();
simpleAlert.sendKeys("text"); // Will fail!

// ✅ CORRECT - Only use sendKeys on prompts
Alert promptAlert = driver.switchTo().alert();
if (promptAlert.getText().contains("Enter")) {
    promptAlert.sendKeys("text");
}
promptAlert.accept();
```

**6. Using dismiss() on Simple Alerts**
```java
// ❌ WRONG - Simple alerts only have accept()
Alert simpleAlert = driver.switchTo().alert();
simpleAlert.dismiss(); // No effect or may cause error

// ✅ CORRECT - Use accept() for simple alerts
alert.accept();
```

**Best Practice - Reusable Alert Handler:**
```java
public void handleAlert(boolean accept, String inputText) {
    try {
        WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
        Alert alert = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent());

        String alertText = alert.getText();
        System.out.println("Alert: " + alertText);

        if (inputText != null && !inputText.isEmpty()) {
            alert.sendKeys(inputText);
        }

        if (accept) {
            alert.accept();
        } else {
            alert.dismiss();
        }
    } catch (Exception e) {
        System.out.println("Error handling alert: " + e.getMessage());
    }
}
```

